&lt;p class="NoteText" style="text-align: justify; tab-stops: 79.55pt;"&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160; &lt;br /&gt;&lt;span style="mso-fareast-font-family: Arial; color: windowtext;"&gt;The TCJA includes significant changes affecting regulated public utilities, such as NY Transco. Substantially all of the provisions of the TCJA are effective for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2017. The TCJA reduces the corporate federal income tax rate from 35% to 21%. The specific provision related to regulated public utilities in the TCJA generally allows the continued deductibility of interest expense, does not allow for full expensing for tax purposes of certain property acquired after September 27, 2017, and continues certain rate normalization requirements for accelerated depreciation benefits.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="NoteText" style="text-align: justify; tab-stops: 79.55pt;"&gt;&lt;span style="mso-fareast-font-family: Arial; color: windowtext;"&gt;In accordance with the accounting rules for income taxes, the tax effects of changes in tax laws are to be recognized in the period in which the law is enacted and deferred tax assets and liabilities are to be re-measured at the enacted tax rate expected to apply when temporary differences are to be realized or settled. In accordance with the accounting rules for regulated operations, the change in deferred taxes was recorded as a regulatory liability at December 31, 2017, for approximately $5.1 million.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="NoteNumber" style="margin-left: .4in; text-indent: -.4in; page-break-after: auto; mso-list: l33 level1 lfo13; tab-stops: list .4in;"&gt;&lt;!-- [if !supportLists]--&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'Arial Bold'; mso-fareast-font-family: 'Arial Bold'; mso-bidi-font-family: 'Arial Bold';"&gt;7.&lt;span style="font-style: normal; font-variant: normal; font-size-adjust: none; font-language-override: normal; font-kerning: auto; font-optical-sizing: auto; font-feature-settings: normal; font-variation-settings: normal; font-stretch: normal; font-size: 7pt; line-height: normal; font-family: 'Times New Roman';"&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;!--[endif]--&gt;Commitments and Contingencies&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="NoteNumber" style="margin-left: .4in; text-align: justify; page-break-after: auto; mso-list: none;"&gt;The Company is subject to various legal proceedings arising out of the ordinary course of its business. The Company does not consider any of such proceedings to be material, individually or in the aggregate, to its business or likely to result in a material adverse effect on its results of operations, financial position, or cash flows.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="NoteNumber" style="margin-left: .4in; text-align: justify; page-break-after: auto; mso-list: none;"&gt;The Company has entered into agreements with entities to provide engineering, procurement, construction and project management services related to the Propel Project. These agreements extend until project completion and acceptance (the expected completion for the Propel Project is in 2030) unless terminated earlier without penalty. The agreements cover approximately 9% of the work related to the Propel Project.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="NoteNumber" style="margin-left: .4in; text-align: justify; text-indent: -.4in; page-break-after: auto; mso-list: l33 level1 lfo13; tab-stops: list .4in;"&gt;&lt;!-- [if !supportLists]--&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'Arial Bold'; mso-fareast-font-family: 'Arial Bold'; mso-bidi-font-family: 'Arial Bold';"&gt;8.&lt;span style="font-style: normal; font-variant: normal; font-size-adjust: none; font-language-override: normal; font-kerning: auto; font-optical-sizing: auto; font-feature-settings: normal; font-variation-settings: normal; font-stretch: normal; font-size: 7pt; line-height: normal; font-family: 'Times New Roman';"&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;!--[endif]--&gt;Related Party Transactions&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="NoteText" style="text-align: justify;"&gt;NYISO Revenues&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="NoteText" style="text-align: justify;"&gt;The Company receives all its revenues from NYISO, which collects the Company&amp;#8217;s revenue requirement from load serving entities in New York, including the Company&amp;#8217;s New York State utility affiliates. Company transmission project costs are allocated to transmission districts or load zones as determined by NYISO and filed with the FERC. Accordingly, revenue collected by NYISO on behalf of the Company is collected from the customers of these affiliates consistent with the allocation methodology.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="NoteText" style="margin-bottom: 0in; margin-top: 0in; mso-margin-bottom-alt: 12.0pt; mso-margin-top-alt: 0in; mso-add-space: auto; text-align: justify;"&gt;Transactions with Affiliated Companies&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="NoteText" style="margin-bottom: 0in; margin-top: 0in; mso-margin-bottom-alt: 12.0pt; mso-margin-top-alt: 0in; mso-add-space: auto; text-align: justify;"&gt;Consolidated Edison Company of New York, Inc. (&amp;#8220;CECONY&amp;#8221;), Central Hudson Gas &amp;amp; Electric Corporation (&amp;#8220;CHG&amp;amp;E&amp;#8221;), National Grid (&amp;#8220;Grid&amp;#8221;), New York State Electric &amp;amp; Gas Corporation (&amp;#8220;NYSEG&amp;#8221;) and Orange &amp;amp; Rockland Utilities, Inc. (&amp;#8220;O&amp;amp;R&amp;#8221;), affiliates of the Company, provide various services to the Company in the ordinary course of business, including executive and administrative, financial (including accounting and tax), and operational and maintenance services on some of its transmission assets. During the six months ended June 30, 2026, the Company recorded $1.4 million of expenses for costs billed by affiliated companies and are included in Operation Expenses, Maintenance Expenses and Taxes Other Than Income Taxes for $0.3, $0.1 and $1.0 million, respectively. During the six months ended June 30, 2025, the Company recorded $1.2 million of expenses for costs billed by affiliated companies and are included in Operation Expenses, Maintenance Expenses and Taxes Other Than Income Taxes for $0.1, $0.1 and $1.0 million, respectively.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="NoteText" style="margin-bottom: 0in; margin-top: 0in; mso-margin-bottom-alt: 12.0pt; mso-margin-top-alt: 0in; mso-add-space: auto; text-align: justify;"&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="NoteText" style="text-align: justify;"&gt;During the six months ended June 30, 2026 and 2025, the Company incurred costs for the Propel Project from CECONY, CHG&amp;amp;E, Grid, NYSEG and O&amp;amp;R, which were charged to Utility plant&lt;span style="mso-fareast-font-family: Arial; color: windowtext;"&gt;. &lt;/span&gt;The amount charged during the six months ended June 30, 2026 and 2025 were $0.9 and $0.8 million, respectively.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="NoteNumber" style="margin-left: .4in; text-align: justify; page-break-after: auto; mso-list: none;"&gt;Accounts payable to affiliated companies totaled $24.2 and $24.0 million as of June 30, 2026 and December 31, 2025, respectively, which included costs related to Utility plant of $8.1 and $8.1 million as of June 30, 2026 and December 31, 2025, respectively.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="NoteNumber" style="margin-left: .4in; text-align: justify; text-indent: -.4in; page-break-after: auto; mso-list: l33 level1 lfo13; tab-stops: list .4in;"&gt;&lt;!-- [if !supportLists]--&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'Arial Bold'; mso-fareast-font-family: 'Arial Bold'; mso-bidi-font-family: 'Arial Bold';"&gt;9.&lt;span style="font-style: normal; font-variant: normal; font-size-adjust: none; font-language-override: normal; font-kerning: auto; font-optical-sizing: auto; font-feature-settings: normal; font-variation-settings: normal; font-stretch: normal; font-size: 7pt; line-height: normal; font-family: 'Times New Roman';"&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;!--[endif]--&gt;Joint Development Agreement&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="NoteText" style="text-align: justify;"&gt;On April 23, 2023, the Company entered into a joint development agreement (the &amp;#8220;JDA&amp;#8221;) with NYPA to develop the Propel Project (refer to Note 1), which had an estimated cost of $2.8 billion. In accordance with the JDA, NYPA currently has a financial obligation of $0.5 billion towards the Propel Project, with the remaining $2.3 billion as the financial obligation of the &lt;span style="mso-fareast-font-family: Arial; color: windowtext;"&gt;Company. NYPA also has a right to increase its investment to 30% of the total cost for the Propel Project.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="NoteText" style="text-align: justify;"&gt;&lt;span style="mso-fareast-font-family: Arial; color: windowtext;"&gt;Additionally, according to the JDA, the financial ownership percentages of the Propel Project, which include all project costs incurred, are based on the financial obligations of NYPA and the Company. As of December 31, 2025, the financial ownership percentages are calculated as 17.8% for NYPA and 82.2% for the Company. In the event that costs incurred and recorded on the Company&amp;#8217;s books are more or less than the amount that should be allocated to maintain the 82.2% financial ownership percentage, the Company will receive from or reimburse NYPA the difference in order to maintain the financial ownership percentages. As of June 30, 2026, the Company has recorded a project cost reimbursement from NYPA in the amount of $10.7 million, which is included in &lt;/span&gt;Miscellaneous current and accrued assets.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="NoteNumber" style="margin-left: .4in; text-align: justify; text-indent: -.4in; page-break-after: auto; mso-list: l33 level1 lfo13; tab-stops: list .4in;"&gt;&lt;!-- [if !supportLists]--&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'Arial Bold'; mso-fareast-font-family: 'Arial Bold'; mso-bidi-font-family: 'Arial Bold';"&gt;10.&lt;span style="font-style: normal; font-variant: normal; font-size-adjust: none; font-language-override: normal; font-kerning: auto; font-optical-sizing: auto; font-feature-settings: normal; font-variation-settings: normal; font-stretch: normal; font-size: 7pt; line-height: normal; font-family: 'Times New Roman';"&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;!--[endif]--&gt;Slot Reservations&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="NoteText" style="text-align: justify;"&gt;&lt;span style="mso-fareast-font-family: Arial; color: windowtext;"&gt;The Company had incurred $10 million of costs associated with the purchase of manufacturing capacity slot reservations for the manufacture of high voltage converter station equipment (the &amp;#8220;Slot Reservations&amp;#8221;). The Slot Reservations were related to the development of solutions in response to the NYISO&amp;#8217;s PPTPP solicitation to address the NYC Offshore Wind Export Public Policy Transmission Need (the &amp;#8220;NYC OSW PPTN&amp;#8221;) as declared by the NYSPSC on June 22, 2023 in Case 22-E-0633. On July 17, 2025, the NYSPSC issued an order in the same docket to withdraw (i.e., cancel) the NYC OSW PPTN effective immediately (&amp;#8220;NYC OSW PPTN Cancellation&amp;#8221;). &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="NoteText" style="text-align: justify;"&gt;&lt;span style="mso-fareast-font-family: Arial; color: windowtext;"&gt;As a result of the NYC OSW PPTN Cancellation, the Company&amp;#8217;s management committed to a plan to sell the Slot Reservations to a third party through a broker. The Slot Reservations are set to expire on November 30, 2026, with the possibility to extend the expiration date. Fair value of the Slot Reservations is based on current market conditions, less the estimated cost to sell. As the resulting net fair value of the Slot Reservations exceeds the carrying value, no loss was recorded. The Company has accounted for the assets, in Miscellaneous Current and Accrued Assets as of June 30, 2026.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="NoteText" style="text-align: justify;"&gt;&lt;span style="mso-fareast-font-family: Arial; color: windowtext;"&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/div&gt;</ferc:NotesToFinancialStatementsTextBlock>
